 ![Global costmap](assets/3-globalcostmap.png)
 
+#### Keep-out zones and virtual walls
+
+A virtual boundary drawn on the map, in the spirit of Nav2's keepout filter ([`keepout_zones.py`](/dimos/mapping/keepout_zones.py)). Publish JSON on the `keepout_zones` stream (`std_msgs/String`):
+
+```json
+{"frame_id": "world", "allowed": [[[x, y], ...]], "keepout": [[[x, y], ...]], "walls": [[[x1, y1], [x2, y2]]]}
+```
+
+- `allowed`: polygons the robot must stay inside. Every cell outside them is marked lethal, unknown cells included, so frontier exploration finds no frontier there.
+- `keepout`: polygons it must stay out of.
+- `walls`: segments it must not cross (every cell a segment touches, so a diagonal wall has no gap).
+
+Known cells inside the edge get a soft margin of rising cost (`keepout_margin_m`, `keepout_margin_cost`), kept below 50 so the Go2 planner's gradient does not read the margin as a wall. A robot standing on a forbidden cell (pushed out, or relocalized) gets a passable band toward the nearest allowed cells (`keepout_escape_slack_m`, `keepout_escape_cost`), so it can plan its way back in. Zones in another frame than the costmap's are placed through the transform tree; if they cannot be placed, the whole costmap is lethal.
+
+The layer changes only the published costmap: the point clouds, the voxel map and anything built from the lidar never see it, so a virtual wall is never matched as a real one. It is not a safety barrier: it holds only while localization does.
+
 ### 4. Navigation Costmap ([`ReplanningAStarPlanner`](/dimos/navigation/go2/replanning_a_star/module.py))
 
 The planner processes the terrain gradient and computes its own planning costmap, preferring safe free paths but willing to path aggressively through tight spaces when it has to.
