 This is essential for sensor fusion where you need to know where the camera was when an image was captured, not where it is now.
 
+### Looking up between samples
+
+By default `get(..., time_point=t)` returns the recorded sample nearest `t` (within `time_tolerance`). Odometry arrives at 10-30 Hz, so the nearest sample can be 15-50 ms away from a camera frame's stamp, which at walking speed is a few centimetres. Pass `interpolate=True` to get the transform at `t` itself: translation is blended linearly and rotation by slerp between the samples either side, on every edge of a chain. It never extrapolates: a time outside the buffered samples, or a bracket wider than `max_gap` seconds, gives `None`. A live caller asking about a time newer than the last sample can wait for the next one with `forward_tolerance`:
+
+```python skip
+pose = tf.get("world", "camera_optical", image.ts, interpolate=True, max_gap=0.2, forward_tolerance=0.1)
+```
+
+The default stays nearest-sample, since many callers look up mount transforms that are published rarely, which interpolation would refuse.
+
+### The buffer's clock
+
+What "now" means to a buffer (the stamp of an identity lookup, the time in a miss's warning) is its `clock`, by default the robot's clock `dimos.utils.clock.now` (the wall clock on a robot, sim time when a simulation publishes its clock). Pass any `() -> float` on the clock your transforms are stamped with, such as a simulation's: `MultiTBuffer(clock=sim_now)` or `TF(stream, clock=sim_now)`.
+
+### Recordings
+
+`dimos.memory.tf.StreamTF` answers the same `get()` over a recording, by the transforms' own stamps whatever time the recorder stored each message at (a simulation running slower than real time can stamp on its own clock while a store keeps another): on first use it scans each stream once for its stamps, then reads windows of it as lookups need them. A recording whose moving edge was kept as an odometry stream can supply it from there: `StreamTF.from_store(store, odom="odom", odom_child_frame="base_link")` takes each odometry pose as the `frame_id -> base_link` edge (an `Odometry` message's own child frame wins), in place of any copy of that edge in `tf`. "Now" for a recording is its latest stamp.
+
