+## Extensions: panels of your own
+
+The built-in panels ship with the cockpit. A panel of your own needs no change to it: the relay's operator puts
+the panel's module in a directory and starts the relay with `--extensions-dir DIR` (or `dimos run ... --local-relay
+<blueprint> --extensions-dir DIR`). The relay serves the directory under `/ext/` and lists its top-level `.js` and
+`.mjs` files in `/api/info`; the cockpit loads them, in that order, before it subscribes to anything, so their panel
+kinds pass the same subscription gate as the built-in ones.
+
+Code only ever comes from that directory. A robot's manifest names panel kinds, never code, the cockpit refuses a
+listed path outside `/ext/`, and an extension cannot replace a built-in kind.
+
+A module exports `register(api)` (or a default function). The `api` carries everything a panel needs, so a module
+can be plain JavaScript with no build step and no imports:
+
+```js
+// battery.js, in the extensions directory
+export function register({ React, registerPanel, PanelFrame, useStoreChannel }) {
+  const h = React.createElement;
+  registerPanel("battery", ({ spec, store }) => {
+    const snap = useStoreChannel(store, spec.channels[0]);
+    const text = snap.slot === null ? "waiting" : `${snap.slot.value.soc} %`;
+    return h(PanelFrame, { spec, children: h("span", null, text) });
+  });
+}
+```
+
+| `api` member | What it is |
+|---|---|
+| `version` | The api's version (1); bumped only when a member changes or goes. |
+| `React` | The cockpit's own React, so hooks work inside the cockpit's tree. |
+| `registerPanel(kind, Component)` | Adds a panel kind. The component gets the same props as a built-in panel: `spec` (id, kind, title, channels, params), `store`, and `teleop`/`session` for sending. |
+| `registerDecoder(encoding, decoder)` | Adds a payload decoder for an encoding of your own (`*.json.vN` decodes without one). |
+| `PanelFrame` | The built-in panels' frame (title bar, fullscreen). |
+| `useStoreChannel(store, ch)` | A channel's latest slot and stats, re-rendering on change. |
+
+In Python, a panel of your own is a `Panel` subclass with its `kind` and its channels:
+
+```python
+from dataclasses import dataclass, field
+from typing import ClassVar
+from dimos.web.cockpit import Channel, Panel, _request_of
+
+@dataclass(frozen=True)
+class Battery(Panel):
+    kind: ClassVar[str] = "battery"
+    title: str = field(default="Battery", kw_only=True)
+
+    def _channels(self):
+        return (Channel("battery", BatteryState, encoding="battery.json.v1", max_hz=1.0),)
+
+    def _channel_requests(self):
+        return tuple(_request_of(c) for c in self._channels())
+```
+
+A module that fails to load is logged in the browser console and named on every panel whose kind is still unknown;
+the rest of the cockpit works as before.
+
 ## Keyboard teleop
 
 Click the teleop panel to arm it. While it is armed:
@@ -241,6 +298,10 @@ Click the teleop panel to arm it. While it is armed:
 
 Releasing the last key sends a zero command, repeated twice over 200 ms, and the panel stays armed. Focus leaving the panel, the window losing focus and the tab being hidden all disarm and zero (Esc disarms by dropping focus), and a disconnect disarms. A key that stays pressed keeps the robot moving: nothing can tell a held key from a stuck one, and the watchdog below only catches silence. Only one viewer can drive a robot at a time. A second viewer that arms gets `teleop_held` until the first one disarms or disconnects.
 
+On a touch screen (a coarse pointer, or from the first touch on any screen) the panel shows a thumb joystick and a STOP button in place of the key cluster. Tap the panel to arm. Up and down drive forward and back, left and right turn, and a full deflection on one axis sends what W, S, A or D would. The joystick shares the keys' stream, limits, cadence and disarm rules, so the safety chain below covers it unchanged. Lifting the thumb (or the browser cancelling the touch) sends a zero at once, like releasing the last key. STOP sends a stop and a zero and disarms. Locking the phone hides the page, which disarms and zeroes like a hidden tab.
+
+A gamepad drives the same way. The panel shows the connected pad's name and what to press. Hold the deadman button (LB by default) and steer with the left stick: up and down drive forward and back, left and right turn, and a full deflection on one axis sends what W, S, A or D would. Pressing the deadman also arms the panel. A dead zone of 15% around the centre keeps a resting stick from creeping. Releasing the deadman sends a zero at once, like lifting the thumb. The STOP button (B / East by default) sends a stop and a zero and disarms, and the deadman has to be pressed again to drive. A pad that disconnects zeroes and disarms. Only pads the browser reports with the standard mapping drive; others are listed but ignored. Browsers deliver pad input only to a visible page, and list a pad only after one of its buttons has been pressed.
+
 Three hops make sure that a broken link stops the robot instead of leaving it moving:
 
 1. The cockpit sends a zero and releases the lease on every disarm (Esc, focus loss, a hidden tab), and disarms locally on a disconnect. This covers a closed or backgrounded tab.
